          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "A combination code is a single classification code that holds two conditions, or a condition and a complication, together. When one exists, it is used in place of separate codes for each part. The reference explains that this is why the diabetes family is so large. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n\nThe retrieved passages name these conditions as coded with combination codes:\n\n- **Diabetes (type 2, under E11):** Diabetes linked with complications such as chronic kidney disease or foot ulcers has combination codes. Some still need a second code for detail, such as the kidney disease stage or the ulcer's site and depth. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n- **Hypertension with organ involvement:** Hypertension that affects the heart, the kidneys, or both moves to combination codes (I11, I12, I13). Secondary hypertension with an identified cause is in I15. (Source: Coding hypertension: I10.)\n\nThe reference also says that when two conditions are linked by the word \"with\" in the index, the classification presumes a link unless the record says they are unrelated. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n\nThe passages did not give a full list of every combination code in the classification. They also did not cover what the codes mean for any particular person's care, and this is not a question for a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
